! jb_af1c F214 "MULTIPLY TEST ROUTINE", sub-block F214C "TESTS OF
! UNCHANGED REGISTERS", pp.138-139 (octal 017731.00-017760.40, the
! block's own exit "B,$+1.").  Continues fp_arith_f214b.strap.
!
! THIS BLOCK ANSWERS THE QUESTION F214A COULD NOT.  F214A checks only
! the exponent and the high fraction, so nothing there says whether
! multiply writes the accumulator's low half -- and the machine has a
! 96-bit accumulator, so it is a fair question.  C2 settles it:
!
!   C2  fills the sign byte, the LEFT word and -- via L(BU),FPK1 --
!       the RIGHT word with ones, multiplies by FPK0 (zero), and then
!       demands "TEST R +1 S": the whole right word STILL ALL ONES.
!
! A multiply that wrote a 96-bit product into IBM 12-107 would put
! zeros through IBM 64-107 and fail that check.  So MULTIPLY LEAVES
! IBM 64-127 ALONE.  C1 is the same test from the other side: clear
! everything, multiply by FPK56, and require the right word to remain
! zero.  Together they pin the behaviour from both polarities, the way
! F212XC and F213C do for their instructions.
!
! WHICH OPERAND EACH CONNECTIVE ACTUALLY LOOKS AT, since it decides
! what the checks mean.  Reading the four truth-table bits as the
! results for (memory, accumulator) = (0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1):
!   CT0101 = 0,1,0,1  ->  the ACCUMULATOR bit; memory is a dummy
!   CT1010 = 1,0,1,0  ->  NOT the accumulator bit; memory is a dummy
!   CT0011 = 0,0,1,1  ->  the MEMORY bit, i.e. the sign byte at word 10
!   CT1100 = 1,1,0,0  ->  NOT the memory bit
! So the L60-63 and R checks read the accumulator and ignore their
! addressed constant entirely, while the SB checks read memory.
!
! TRANSCRIPTION NOTE: C2's first check has its two columns disagree.
! The source column reads "CT1010%BU,4¤,FPK1,64" but the printed code
! is "22005.00 80 004040.25 70", and 022005 is FPK0, not FPK1 (022006).
! Transcribed to match the PRINTED CODE, which is what this verifier
! reproduces.  It cannot change the test either way: CT1010's result
! does not depend on the memory operand at all, per the table above, so
! FPK0 and FPK1 give the same answer.  The row immediately after it
! prints 22006 and does read FPK1, which is why the disagreement is
! visible at all.

! -- SSIP framework substitutes: SERS (octal 1304) -> FAIL halt,
! -- SEN (octal 1310) -> scratch.
        SLC, (8)1304.0
SERS    BEW, $
        SLC, (8)1310.0
SEN     DD(BU,64,8), 0

! -- constants (pool p.176) --
        SLC, (8)22005.0
FPK0    DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000000000
FPK1    DD(BU,64,8), (8)1777777777777777777777
        SLC, (8)22075.0
FPK56   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0002024713560247135627

        SLC, (8)17731.0
F214C   CM0000(BU,8), $SB                    ! F214C1 - 0 s remain 0
        C0000(BU,64), FPK0, 64
        C0000(BU,64), FPK0
        *(U), FPK56
        CT0101(BU,4), FPK0, 64               ! test L60-63 +0
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT0101(BU,64), FPK0                  ! test R +0
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT0011(BU,4), $SB                    ! test SB0-3 +0
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT0011(BU,3), $SB+.5                 ! test SB 5-7 +0
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        B, $+.32                             ! F214C2 - 1 s remain 1 s
        L(BU), FPK1                          ! fills the RIGHT word with ones
        CM1111(BU,8), $SB
        C1111(BU,64), FPK1, 64               ! fills the LEFT word with ones
        *(U), FPK0
        CT1010(BU,4), FPK0, 64               ! test L60-63 +1 s  (see note)
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT1010(BU,64), FPK1                  ! test R +1 s -- the load-bearing row
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT1100(BU,4), $SB                    ! test SB 0-3 +1 s
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT1100(BU,3), $SB+.5                 ! test SB5-7 +1 s
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
PASS    BEW, $                               ! 017760.40: the block's exit "B,$+1."
        END, F214C
